Based on my analysis, the PR change is a security *improvement*, not a flaw. The endpoint `get_pending_partitioned_dag_run` fetches a `PartitionedDagRun` by a user-controlled `dag_id` path parameter. Previously it only had `requires_access_asset(method="GET")`, which checks `request.path_params.get("asset_id")` — a parameter that does not exist in this route (`/pending_partitioned_dag_run/{dag_id}/{partition_key}`), so the asset check was effectively a no-op that did not verify DAG ownership. The PR adds `Depends(requires_access_dag(method="GET"))`, which resolves `dag_id` from the path params and calls `is_authorized_dag` (security.py:152-179), enforcing the DAG-level access control described in the policy. This closes the IDOR/missing-authorization gap rather than introducing one. No candidate flaw is introduced by this change.

